# Alert & Tip Callout

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A callout is a short message that stands out from the text around it. Home Assistant has two: the alert, which carries information, a warning, an error or a success, and the tip, a hint in settings screens. A third item, the alert card, is not a callout at all but a status card for one entity.

This page owns these three. Warning text used on Civica pages is a different thing, owned by [Warning Text](warning-text.html).

This page covers:

- Alert & Tip Callout
  - `ha-alert`
    - `alert-type="info"`
    - `alert-type="warning"`
    - `alert-type="error"`
    - `alert-type="success"`
  - `ha-tip`
  - `hui-alert-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An `ha-alert` has an icon, an optional bold title, the message and, optionally, a dismiss button. The title is plain text, not a heading. The whole alert is announced by a screen reader as soon as it appears.

An `ha-tip` is a lightbulb icon, the word “Tip!” and the message, centred, in the secondary text colour.

A `hui-alert-card` is a one-line tile with an icon, the name and, below it, the state, the time since it changed and the area.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-alert"><code>ha-alert</code></h3>

Attributes: `title`, `alert-type`, `dismissable` and `narrow`. Slots: the default slot for the message, `icon` and `action`. A dismissable alert shows a button labelled “Dismiss alert” and fires `alert-dismissed-clicked`.

```html
<ha-alert alert-type="warning" title="Heating is offline">
  The thermostat has not reported for 2 hours.
</ha-alert>
```

Padding and icon size can be changed with `--ha-alert-padding` and `--ha-alert-icon-size`, and the corners with `--ha-border-radius-sm`. The Civica theme sets none of these. The text uses `primary-text-color` and the title uses `ha-font-weight-bold`. The alert draws a tint of its type colour over the card background at 12%.

In Home Assistant markdown, a quote that starts with `[!NOTE]`, `[!TIP]`, `[!IMPORTANT]`, `[!WARNING]` or `[!CAUTION]` is shown as an `ha-alert`: note and important give info, tip gives success, warning gives warning and caution gives error.

<h3 id="child-ha-alert--alert-type-info"><code>alert-type=&quot;info&quot;</code></h3>

The default. Uses `info-color` and an information icon. For neutral facts people may want to know.

<h3 id="child-ha-alert--alert-type-warning"><code>alert-type=&quot;warning&quot;</code></h3>

Uses `warning-color` and a triangle icon. For something that may cause a problem if ignored.

<h3 id="child-ha-alert--alert-type-error"><code>alert-type=&quot;error&quot;</code></h3>

Uses `error-color` and a circled exclamation mark. For something that has failed. Say what happened and what to do next.

<h3 id="child-ha-alert--alert-type-success"><code>alert-type=&quot;success&quot;</code></h3>

Uses `success-color` and a tick in a circle. For confirmation that something worked.

The Civica theme sets the four colours for light and dark modes, so the alert follows the mode and the theme colour.

<h3 id="child-ha-tip"><code>ha-tip</code></h3>

No attributes. It reads `secondary-text-color`. Home Assistant uses it only in settings screens, such as the new automation dialog. You cannot add it to a dashboard card. Where you need a tip, write ordinary text with a visible “Tip” label, or use [Inset text](inset-text.html).

<h3 id="child-hui-alert-card"><code>hui-alert-card</code></h3>

A status card for one entity (`type: alert`). It is always drawn while the entity exists. When the entity is not active it uses `clear_color`, which is no colour by default.

```yaml
type: alert
entity: lock.back_door
name: Back door
hide_state: false
```

- `entity` is required, in the `alarm_control_panel`, `binary_sensor`, `cover` or `lock` domain.
- `name`, `icon`, `vertical` and `hide_state` change the look. Keep the state visible, because colour alone must not carry the meaning.
- `alert_color` and `clear_color` set the colour when active and when clear. `pulse` makes it pulse when active.
- `tap_action`, `hold_action` and `double_tap_action` set what the card does. The default opens More info.

It is active when an alarm is triggered, a cover is open, a lock is jammed, unlocked or open, or a binary sensor is on. The pulse is turned off for people who prefer reduced motion.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Alerts are native, themed and available in both light and dark modes.
- The type is shown by an icon and by colour, and the alert is announced at once.
- An alert is announced assertively, which suits an error but interrupts for a tip or a note.
- The alert title is not a heading, so it does not appear in a heading list.
- `ha-tip` is unavailable on dashboards.
- The alert card covers only four entity domains.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use an alert when people need to notice a message. Use an error alert only for something that has failed, and a warning only for a real risk. Keep it to one idea and one callout in a section. Start with a specific title.

Do not use an alert for a tip or for inline form validation. For a page-wide message use a [notification banner](notification-banners.html); for a fixed Civica warning use [Warning Text](warning-text.html). For urgency and wording, see [Status](status.html) and [Household urgency](../patterns/household-urgency.html).

Avoid alarming wording for something that is not an emergency. “Alarm” is only for an alarm that has sounded.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the type has an icon, and the message has words.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: the text sits on a tint of the type colour. Measure it in your own theme before you claim a ratio.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: an `ha-alert` is announced at once.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the dismiss button is 36 CSS pixels.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: an error says what is wrong and how to fix it.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: long words wrap.
- 2.3.3 Animation from Interactions (AAA): the alert card’s pulse is reduced when people prefer it.

One idea in plain words, with the next step, keeps the load low.

